{"causes":["The rise of Romanticism in the early 19th century encouraged the collection and publication of folk tales.","The Brothers Grimm sought to preserve German folklore amidst the growing nationalism of their time.","Oral traditions and fairy tales had been passed down through generations, reflecting cultural values and societal norms."],"effects":["The Frog Prince became a foundational text in the study of fairy tales and folklore, influencing literary scholarship.","It inspired numerous adaptations in various media, including theater, film, and literature, extending its impact across cultures.","The tale contributed to the establishment of a distinct German cultural identity during the 19th century."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"brothers-grimm","sourceName":"Brothers Grimm","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"the-frog-prince","targetName":"The Frog Prince","context":"In 1812, the Brothers Grimm published The Frog Prince as part of their collection of folk tales."},{"sourceSlug":"the-frog-prince","sourceName":"The Frog Prince","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"disney-fairy-tales","targetName":"Disney Fairy Tales","context":"The Frog Prince influenced Disney adaptations, particularly in the portrayal of characters and themes in animated films."},{"sourceSlug":"the-frog-prince","sourceName":"The Frog Prince","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"german-folk-tales","targetName":"German Folk Tales","context":"The Frog Prince is a key component of the broader collection of German Folk Tales, reflecting the cultural heritage of Germany."},{"sourceSlug":"grimm-brothers-legacy","sourceName":"Grimm Brothers' Legacy","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"the-frog-prince","targetName":"The Frog Prince","context":"The legacy of the Brothers Grimm's commitment to preserving folklore directly transmitted The Frog Prince to future generations."},{"sourceSlug":"romanticism","sourceName":"Romanticism","verb":"FRAMES","targetSlug":"the-frog-prince","targetName":"The Frog Prince","context":"The Frog Prince was framed within the Romantic movement's emphasis on folklore and national identity in the early 19th century."}],"places":[{"name":"Hanau, Germany","role":"Birthplace of the Brothers Grimm"},{"name":"Berlin, Germany","role":"Cultural center of publication"},{"name":"Frankfurt, Germany","role":"Major distribution hub for fairy tales"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Frog Prince","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-frog-prince"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11850","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Frog_Prince"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Frog Prince contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Frog Prince (German: Der Froschkönig oder der eiserne Heinrich, 'The Frog King, or Iron Henry') is a German fairy tale first published by the Brothers Grimm in Kinder- und Hausmärchen ('Children's and Household Tales') as tale KHM 1 — the very first tale in the Grimm collection — in its first edition (1812). The tale narrates the story of a princess who drops her golden ball into a well; a frog retrieves the ball in exchange for the promise that she will let him eat from her plate, sleep in her bed, and be her companion; the princess reluctantly fulfils the promise but throws the frog against the wall (in the 1812 original) or kisses him (in many later adaptations) — whereupon the frog is transformed into a handsome prince.

The Frog Prince is classified as ATU type 440 ('The Frog King') and exists in related versions across European, American, and Asian folk traditions. The tale's most famous cultural transformation is from 'throwing the frog against the wall' (the original Grimm version — the violence of the princess's disgust breaks the spell) to 'kissing the frog' (the modern popular version — the princess's willingness to overcome her revulsion) — a shift that fundamentally transforms the moral of the story and demonstrates how fairy tale meanings change in transmission and adaptation.

The Frog Prince occupies a central position in psychoanalytic fairy tale analysis — Bruno Bettelheim's The Uses of Enchantment (1976) reads the tale as a narrative of adolescent sexual awakening and the princess's need to overcome her revulsion at male sexuality before achieving mature love. The tale's placement as the first tale in the Grimm collection (KHM 1) has been taken to signal its importance as a paradigmatic tale; and its central image — the transformation of the disgusting into the beautiful through the fulfilment of a promise — has made it one of the most analysed of all European fairy tales.","subjects":["German Literature","19th Century","Fairy Tale","Brothers Grimm","Folk Literature","Children's Literature","Psychoanalysis","Animal Bridegroom"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Frog Prince significantly shaped the study of folklore, influencing both cultural identity in Germany and the broader fairy tale genre worldwide.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
